const body = `<h1>2.8a — APIs &amp; Libraries: Reading the Docs Like a Pro</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You have been calling methods for weeks: <code>Math.random()</code>,
        <code>name.length()</code>, <code>scanner.nextLine()</code>. Every one of those
        was written by someone else and handed to you through a <strong>library</strong>.
        The contract that tells you how to use someone else's code is called an
        <strong>API</strong>, an Application Programming Interface. Today you learn to
        read that contract yourself, using the exact sheet you will hold in your hands
        during the AP exam.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>What is on your desk during the exam.</strong> The Java Quick Reference
        (JQR) is a two-sided sheet listing every library method the exam may test: String,
        Math, Integer, ArrayList, File, Scanner, and Object. You get it in the exam room.
        Reading it fast is a skill, and today starts the training. Open the
        <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">JQR page</a> in another tab. Print it
        if you can. This is your new sidearm.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 1: Three Rows, Three Answers</h3>
        <p>Find the String Class table on the JQR. Answer these using ONLY the sheet,
        no compiler, no notes. Write your answers, then verify each with a program:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>What does <code>"dungeon".substring(3)</code> return?</li>
            <li>What does <code>"dragon".indexOf("drag")</code> return? What about
            <code>"dragon".indexOf("onx")</code>?</li>
            <li>According to the sheet, what EXACTLY does <code>substring(from, to)</code>
            say about the end index? Quote the sentence. Did you remember it correctly
            from Unit 2, or did you learn something new?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 2: Method You Have Never Used</h3>
        <p>Scan the Integer Class table. There is a method there you have never called.
        It turns a String into an int.</p>
        <ol>
            <li>What is its full signature, exactly as printed?</li>
            <li>Write one line that uses it to turn <code>"42"</code> into the number 42.
            Store the result and print it. Then make it fail: try it on
            <code>"forty-two"</code> and read the error.</li>
            <li>Your RPG saves the game by writing stats as text. Why is this method the
            bridge between saving and loading?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 3: The Math Table Speed Run</h3>
        <p>Find the Math Class table. Without notes, answer:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>What type does <code>Math.pow(2, 10)</code> return? Not the value. The TYPE.
            Check the sheet, then prove it with code: what does
            <code>int x = Math.pow(2, 10);</code> do?</li>
            <li>What are the two <code>abs</code> methods listed? Why are there two?</li>
            <li>Write the damage formula for a fire spell: base 6, doubled for every
            torch the player holds (0 to 3 torches). Use <code>Math.pow</code>. Cast the
            result to int. Print damage for each torch count.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>"API" gets used loosely. Based on what you did today, write your own
            definition in one sentence. Then compare it to the
            <a href="../docs/unit-02/api-and-libraries.html">API docs page</a>.</li>
            <li>The JQR lists <code>int compareTo(String other)</code>. Describe, from
            the sheet alone, the three possible kinds of return value and what each means.</li>
            <li>Why does the exam give you this sheet at all? What does that tell you
            about what College Board thinks matters in programming?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Locate any class's method table on the JQR in under 30 seconds</li>
            <li>Translate a signature like <code>static int parseInt(String s)</code> into
            plain English: what goes in, what comes out, static or not</li>
            <li>Use a method you found on the sheet, without ever seeing an example of it</li>
            <li>Explain what a library is and what an API is, in your own words</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">The Java Quick Reference</a> — your exam sheet, full text</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/api-and-libraries.html">APIs &amp; Libraries</a> — vocabulary, javadoc, how libraries fit together</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">The String Class</a> — full method reference with RPG examples</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
